</table>
<p id="S5.Thmresult1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmresult1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where we define the potential <math id="S5.Thmresult1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\mathrm{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\mathrm{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.Thmresult1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}^{L})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}^{L})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, its extremizers and its covariances as:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi,z)=-\frac{1}{2}(y-\sigma(\chi)^{\top}z)^{2}+\sum_{\ell}^{L}\log\mathcal{N}(\chi_{\ell};\gamma_{\ell},V_{k})+\sum_{\ell}^{L}\log\mathcal{N}(z_{\ell};\omega_{\ell},V_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ω</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi,z)=-\frac{1}{2}(y-\sigma(\chi)^{\top}z)^{2}+\sum_{\ell}^{L}\log\mathcal{N}(\chi_{\ell};\gamma_{\ell},V_{k})+\sum_{\ell}^{L}\log\mathcal{N}(z_{\ell};\omega_{\ell},V_{v})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\chi^{\prime},z^{\prime}=\argmax_{\chi,z}\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi,z)\in(\mathbb{R}^{L})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">out</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\chi^{\prime},z^{\prime}=\argmax_{\chi,z}\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi,z)\in(\mathbb{R}^{L})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cov\left(\chi_{\ell}\right)=-\left(\left(\nabla^{2}\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi^{\prime},z^{\prime})\right)^{-1}\right)_{\chi_{\ell}}\in\mathbb{R}\ ,\hskip 18.49988pt\cov\left(z_{\ell}\right)=-\left(\left(\nabla^{2}\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi^{\prime},z^{\prime})\right)^{-1}\right)_{z_{\ell}}\in\mathbb{R}\ ." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">out</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.460em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="2.017em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">out</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">χ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.460em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cov\left(\chi_{\ell}\right)=-\left(\left(\nabla^{2}\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi^{\prime},z^{\prime})\right)^{-1}\right)_{\chi_{\ell}}\in\mathbb{R}\ ,\hskip 18.49988pt\cov\left(z_{\ell}\right)=-\left(\left(\nabla^{2}\psi_{\mathrm{out}}(\chi^{\prime},z^{\prime})\right)^{-1}\right)_{z_{\ell}}\in\mathbb{R}\ .</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The derivation of this result is given in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Asymptotic characterization at finite sampling ratio: replica ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. We simplified it assuming diagonal order parameters, which is analogous to the manifold assumption made in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Comparison of softmax with alternatives in population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Taking the limit <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> we checked that we recover the expression of the population risk of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4.4. ‣ Optimization dynamics and manifold assumption. ‣ 4.2 Expression of the population risk for arbitrary activation functions ‣ 4 Comparison of softmax with alternatives in population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, and in particular <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the value of <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> derived in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Comparison between linear and softmax attentions ‣ 4 Comparison of softmax with alternatives in population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> for the population risk. While our derivation is based on the non-rigorous replica method, we expect that a formal proof could be established under the so-called replicon condition along the lines of recent progress in proof techniques from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vilucchio et al. (2025)</a></cite> that is able to deal with minimization of intrinsically non-convex objectives for single-index models. Extending this proof to multi-index models, such as (<a href="#S5.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 5 Performance of the attention at finite sample complexity ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), is a technical challenge left for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As in the population result of <a href="#S4.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4.2 Expression of the population risk for arbitrary activation functions ‣ 4 Comparison of softmax with alternatives in population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, the high-dimensional analysis shows that the risk depends only on a few order parameters. Although the resulting expressions are cumbersome, it is important to emphasize that these quantities are deterministic and independent of the diverging dimensions <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, in contrast to the original risk (<a href="#S5.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 5 Performance of the attention at finite sample complexity ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). Thus the result provides an implementable formula for the performance of ERMs. In practice, the minimum over the set <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by running the fixed point method from several initializations, typically random (so-called <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uninformed</span>) and <span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">informed</span> ones corresponding to (partial) alignment of <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21936v1/att_rOpt.svg" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/178;" width="476" height="178"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
Test error of the trained attention (linear vs. softmax) across different tasks and signal strengths <math id="S5.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S5.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Linear attention is shown in red and softmax in blue. Solid lines indicate <math id="S5.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> at finite <math id="S5.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (Result <a href="#S5.Thmresult1" title="Result 5.1 (Test risk for attention-based predictors). ‣ 5 Performance of the attention at finite sample complexity ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), while markers represent the test error of an ERM obtained via a local optimization method with <math id="S5.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{ND}=10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{ND}=10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. The regularizations <math id="S5.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are tuned by grid search to minimize the test error. Dotted and dashed lines correspond to the value of <math id="S5.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{E}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> in the infinite-<math id="S5.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> limit (see closed-formed formulas in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Bayes risk and optimality of softmax ‣ 4 Comparison of softmax with alternatives in population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> for softmax and Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2.SSS7" title="A.2.7 Minimizer for the linear attention ‣ A.2 Risk of the attention layer on population loss ‣ Appendix A Population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2.7</span></a> for linear). The Bayes-optimal risk is shown in black (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Bayes-optimal error at finite sample complexity 𝛼 ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for a discussion on its discontinuity). Appendices <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Details on the numerics ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>-<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional figures ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> include more experimental details and results.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude by a few <span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">computational</span> remarks. Because the empirical risk (<a href="#S5.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 5 Performance of the attention at finite sample complexity ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) is non-convex, it is not guaranteed a priori that optimization algorithms can find global minima.
To assess the effect of non-convexity, we rely on numerical simulations shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5 Performance of the attention at finite sample complexity ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. More precisely, we compare the prediction of Result <a href="#S5.Thmresult1" title="Result 5.1 (Test risk for attention-based predictors). ‣ 5 Performance of the attention at finite sample complexity ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> (here, uninformed and informed initializations give the same result) to the outcome of running a local optimization algorithm (specifically, a quasi-Newton method) on the risk (<a href="#S5.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 5 Performance of the attention at finite sample complexity ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), starting from a random initialization, for finite but large <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first note that the agreement between both is excellent. This suggests that potential bad local minima in the optimization landscape are avoided, at least for the appropriate regularization strength. Analyzing this landscape is an interesting open question.
Furthermore, the softmax attention has lower error than the linear attention across all the tested hyperparameters, which shows that the benefits of softmax extend beyond the population risk analysis of Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Comparison of softmax with alternatives in population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to statistical and computational advantages on the empirical risk. We finally note that in this case the softmax is no longer Bayes-optimal but the gap to the Bayes risk closes as <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> grows, as expected from our analysis in <a href="#S4" title="4 Comparison of softmax with alternatives in population risk ‣ Statistical Advantage of Softmax Attention: Insights from Single-Location Regression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
